1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Welcome to the commentary for this piece. We start with 'Jabberwocky'. While it often feels like mere nonsense, notice how Carroll uses the poem as a linguistic palette cleanser. He’s preparing the audience for a world where language—and therefore logic—is fluid.

2
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The choice of the 'vorpal sword' is significant. It’s an invented word, much like the world of Wonderland itself. It signifies that the weapons we use to fight our battles in this world are as fabricated and surreal as the enemies we face.

3
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:30,000
We arrive at the tea-party. This is, in my view, the most claustrophobic sequence. Notice the pacing; it’s frantic, erratic, and deeply uncomfortable. The Dormouse being used as a cushion isn't just dark humor; it’s a commentary on how we treat those who are ‘asleep’ or passive in our societal structures.

4
00:02:31,000 --> 00:03:15,000
When Alice sits down, she expects a social contract. She expects the norms of Victorian dining. The Hatter’s refusal to honor that contract—‘No room! No room!’—is the first indication that Alice’s rationality has no currency here. She is attempting to pay with gold in a kingdom that only accepts lead.

5
00:03:16,000 --> 00:04:00,000
The Raven and the Writing-Desk. Why is it a riddle? Because a riddle requires a solution. By refusing to give one, the Hatter forces Alice into a state of intellectual suspension. It’s a brilliant way to keep the protagonist—and the audience—off-balance.

6
00:04:01,000 --> 00:05:30,000
The watch subplot is fascinating. The Hatter doesn't just treat time as a measure; he treats it as an adversary. When he says, ‘I told you butter wouldn't suit the works,’ he’s acknowledging that he tried to force the mechanics of the real world into his fantasy, and it failed. It’s a tragic moment masked by comedy.

7
00:05:31,000 --> 00:07:30,000
Let’s discuss the ‘Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Bat’ sequence. It’s a parody of the classic nursery rhyme. Carroll often used parody to show how children are taught to recite dogma without understanding it. By twisting it, he’s highlighting the absurdity of rote memorization.

8
00:07:31,000 --> 00:09:00,000
The transition to the Treacle Well story—this is a pivotal moment of ‘boredom’ within the madness. Note how the Dormouse’s story is fragmented. It’s designed to be frustrating. It mimics the sensation of listening to a lecture you don’t understand, which perfectly grounds Alice’s frustration.

9
00:09:01,000 --> 00:12:00,000
We move into the Garden scene. The Rose-tree painters, Five, Seven, and Two. They are the ultimate bureaucrats. They are so terrified of the Queen’s arbitrary justice that they sacrifice their own agency to perform a meaningless task. It’s a haunting look at how fear dictates labor.

10
00:12:01,000 --> 00:15:00,000
The Procession is a masterclass in visual hierarchy. We see the clubs, diamonds, and hearts. It’s not just a march; it’s a display of the caste system. Notice how Alice stands still. She refuses to lie down. This is the moment she transitions from a passive observer to a witness of the regime.

11
00:15:01,000 --> 00:18:00,000
The Queen’s screaming of ‘Off with her head!’ is, frankly, the only way she knows how to interact with the world. She lacks the depth to communicate, so she relies on elimination. It’s a powerful metaphor for leaders who govern through fear rather than engagement.

12
00:18:01,000 --> 00:20:00,000
The croquet game. Look at the flamingo. It’s a tool, but it has its own will. Alice cannot control the game because the tools of the game are alive. This represents the chaos of trying to control one’s own destiny in a world where the variables—the flamingo, the hedgehog, the soldiers—are constantly shifting.

13
00:20:01,000 --> 00:23:00,000
The Cheshire Cat’s grin appearing in the air. This is the most surreal moment because it’s the most honest. The Cat doesn't care about the game, the Queen, or the cards. It exists as a pure observer. In many ways, the Cat is the voice of the author, winking at the audience from the sidelines.

14
00:23:01,000 --> 00:26:00,000
As the game ends and twilight sets in, notice the change in mood. The frantic energy of the ‘daytime’ madness is replaced by the eerie calm of the ‘evening’ trial preparation. The cards are shuffling, literally and figuratively. The system is resetting itself for the next act of absurdity.

15
00:26:01,000 --> 00:29:58,000
We close with the final songs. These aren't just filler; they are the rhythmic heartbeat of the narrative. They ground us in the nonsense one last time before we return to reality. Thank you for listening to this commentary. It was a pleasure to dissect this descent into the looking glass with you.